---
/**
 * What each manufacturer publishes about THIS preparation, on the page about this preparation.
 *
 * WHY HERE AND NOT ONLY ON THE COMPARISON PAGE. /choosing/who-publishes-the-composition/ already
 * holds this measurement, including a per-preparation table. It is the right page for the method
 * and the wrong page for the question: a reader arrives asking about one preparation, and Bing's
 * own per-page figures say the formulation monographs earn four times the impressions of any other
 * collection while the comparison page earns none. The facts were published where nobody was
 * standing.
 *
 * NO NEW CLAIM IS MADE HERE. Every value is read from src/data/brand-disclosure.json, which is the
 * same dataset the comparison page renders and which is published whole at /brand-disclosure.json
 * with the URL of every page read. This component re-presents it beside the formulary entry it is
 * measured against, which is the comparison a manufacturer cannot publish about itself.
 *
 * THE FIVE RULES ARE GUIDES/CHOOSING-EQUIPMENT'S, and they are rules, not preferences:
 *   1. The conflict of interest is disclosed in the section, not in a footer.
 *   2. Organised by preparation, never brand against brand. One preparation per page does that.
 *   3. Every cell is the company's own published state with the date it was read. A company that
 *      published nothing is stated as publishing nothing, never left blank.
 *   4. No score, no rank, no winner. The table is ALPHABETICAL by company, which is deliberate:
 *      sorting by disclosure would be a ranking whatever the heading said.
 *   5. A standing correction offer, and our own entry in the table on the same terms.
 *
 * WHY THE PRODUCT URLS ARE NOT LINKED. The dataset records the exact URL read for every row and
 * /brand-disclosure.json publishes them, so the evidence is available and a named company can
 * check its own row. Linking them from 63 pages would put roughly 200 outbound links to other
 * companies' product pages under scripts/linkcheck.mjs, which would then fail whenever any of
 * those companies reorganised its catalogue. A gate that breaks on someone else's deploy is a gate
 * that gets switched off.
 */
import disclosure from '../data/brand-disclosure.json';
import { rel } from '../lib/site';

interface Props { kind: string; slug: string }
const { kind, slug } = Astro.props;

const doc = disclosure as any;
const rec = kind === 'formulation'
  ? (doc.preparations ?? []).find((p: any) => p.slug === slug)
  : null;

/** The dataset's own floor: fewer than three companies is an anecdote, not a comparison. */
const MIN = doc.minCompaniesForAPreparationRow ?? 3;
const names = new Map<string, any>((doc.companies ?? []).map((c: any) => [c.id, c]));

const STATE: Record<string, string> = {
  quantities: 'Ingredients and the quantity of each',
  list: 'An ingredient list, without quantities',
  neither: 'Neither an ingredient list nor quantities',
};

const rows = rec
  ? Object.entries(rec.companies as Record<string, any>)
    .map(([id, v]) => ({
      id,
      name: names.get(id)?.name ?? id,
      ours: Boolean(names.get(id)?.ours),
      state: v.state,
      /*
       * A company can have several pages for one preparation in different states, which is true of
       * 5 of the 353 rows. Saying "publishes an ingredient list" of a company where one page of
       * three does would be a claim the data does not support, so the count travels with the cell
       * wherever the states disagree.
       */
      mixed: v.pages !== v.pagesInThatState,
      pages: v.pages,
      pagesInThatState: v.pagesInThatState,
      readOn: v.readOn,
    }))
    .sort((a, b) => a.name.localeCompare(b.name, 'en'))
  : [];

const show = Boolean(rec) && rows.length >= MIN;
const oursHere = rows.find((r) => r.ours);
const readDates = [...new Set(rows.map((r) => r.readOn).filter(Boolean))].sort();
const dateSpan = readDates.length > 1
  ? `${readDates[0]} to ${readDates[readDates.length - 1]}`
  : readDates[0];
const fmtDate = (iso: string) => new Date(`${iso}T00:00:00Z`).toLocaleDateString('en-GB', { day: 'numeric', month: 'long', year: 'numeric', timeZone: 'UTC' });
---
{show && (
  <section class="disclosure" aria-labelledby="disclosure-heading">
    <h2 id="disclosure-heading">What manufacturers publish about this preparation</h2>

    <p class="disclosure-note">
      <strong>Disclosure first.</strong> This site is published by Age Ayurveda, which sells
      Ayurvedic products{oursHere ? <> and sells this preparation, so it is in the table below on the
      same terms as everyone else</> : <>, though not this preparation</>}. Every cell states what
      that company's own product page carried when it was read, not our judgement of it. There is no
      score, no rank and no winner here, and the table is in alphabetical order for that reason.
    </p>

    <p class="disclosure-note">
      The formulary entry above fixes the ingredients and the quantity of each. Whether you can
      check a particular product against it depends on what its manufacturer publishes. Of the
      {' '}{rows.length} companies surveyed that sell a product under this name, this is what their
      pages carried when they were read{dateSpan ? <> on {fmtDate(readDates[0])}</> : ''}.
    </p>

    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Company</th>
            <th scope="col">What its product page publishes</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {rows.map((r) => (
            <tr>
              <td>
                {r.name}
                {r.ours && <> <span class="disclosure-note">(this site's publisher)</span></>}
              </td>
              <td>
                {STATE[r.state] ?? r.state}
                {r.mixed && (
                  <> <span class="disclosure-note">
                    (on {r.pagesInThatState} of its {r.pages} pages for this preparation; the others differ)
                  </span></>
                )}
              </td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    {/*
      * The sentence that keeps the table honest, and it is not a disclaimer bolted on.
      *
      * Publishing no composition on a website is a disclosure practice. It is not evidence about
      * what is in the bottle, and the printed label is a different and regulated surface. A reader
      * who takes "publishes neither" to mean "does not follow the formulary" has been misled by
      * this page, so the page says so before they can.
      */}
    <p class="disclosure-note">
      This measures what each company publishes, not the quality of anything it makes. A company
      that publishes nothing here may make an excellent preparation, and the label in the box is a
      separate, regulated surface that is not what was read. What disclosure changes is whether a
      reader, a pharmacist or an answer engine can check a product against the formulary entry it
      claims.
    </p>

    <p class="disclosure-note">
      Method, the full counts across every company surveyed, and what this measurement got wrong
      the first time: <a href={rel('/choosing/who-publishes-the-composition/')}>which manufacturers
      publish what is in the bottle</a>. The URL of every page read is recorded in
      {' '}<a href={rel('/brand-disclosure.json')}>brand-disclosure.json</a>. If a row here is
      wrong, including where the correction favours a company other than ours, tell us and we will
      fix it and say that we did.
    </p>
  </section>
)}
